{
  "requirement": "`AgentConfig` and its backend-specific configuration types implement Serde\nserialization and deserialization. An application can embed them in its own\nconfiguration types; serializing and deserializing a supported configuration\npreserves its settings.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The structural obligation is the clause \"`AgentConfig` and its backend-specific configuration types implement Serde serialization and deserialization\". An alternative that only wrote JSON by hand, without Serde traits, would break that clause, because an application could not embed these types in its own Serde-derived configuration types. The clause is therefore structural. The other clauses are behavioral: \"serializing and deserializing a supported configuration preserves its settings\" is a round-trip property. The test criterion covers that property, not this review.\n\nThe root `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/config.rs#enum[AgentConfig]` derives `Serialize, Deserialize`, with `#[serde(tag = \"backend\", rename_all = \"lowercase\")]`. Its variants hold `BedrockConfig`, `OpenAiConfig` and `OllamaConfig`, which a reviewer reaches by following the types the marked root uses. Each of those also derives `Serialize, Deserialize` in the same file: `struct[BedrockConfig]`, `struct[OpenAiConfig]` and `struct[OllamaConfig]`. So do the nested types they use: `struct[BedrockCredentials]`, `struct[SecretString]` (a transparent newtype) and `enum[OptionValue]`. All of these are reached through the marked root, so they need no separate {impl} markers. No other root needs a reference, and no acknowledgments apply.\n\nbehavioral: The requirement asks for Serde serialization and deserialization of `AgentConfig` and its backend config types, and for a round trip that preserves settings. It also says an application can embed them in its own configuration types.\n\n`mod[tests]/fn[all_backends_preserve_nested_settings_and_credentials]` in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/config.rs` covers the round trip:\n- It uses fully populated Bedrock, OpenAI and Ollama JSON values.\n- It deserializes each into `AgentConfig` and asserts the re-serialized value equals the input exactly.\n- It then round-trips through a JSON string and asserts the copy equals the original config.\n- `AgentConfig` wraps `BedrockConfig`, `OpenAiConfig` and `OllamaConfig`, so those types are exercised through it.\n- The Bedrock case covers `BedrockCredentials`, including `session_token`.\n- The inputs also cover nested option maps and arrays and every `OptionValue` variant: string, bool, integer, float, array and map.\n- The `backend` tag is checked by the equality assertion on every case.\n\n`mod[tests]/fn[configuration_debug_hides_credentials]` adds `SecretString` serialization and deserialization. It also checks that an unknown field is rejected.\n\nThe embedding obligation is satisfied by the Serde derives on `AgentConfig` and the config types, and the round-trip tests exercise those derives. Embedding adds no conditional or transformation of its own. I found no required behavior that the tests would let a violation through on.\n\nNo gaps found.",
