        "rationale": "The structural obligation is the clause \"`AgentConfig` and its backend-specific configuration types implement Serde serialization and deserialization\". An alternative that only wrote JSON by hand, without Serde traits, would break that clause, because an application could not embed these types in its own Serde-derived configuration types. The clause is therefore structural. The other clauses are behavioral: \"serializing and deserializing a supported configuration preserves its settings\" is a round-trip property. The test criterion covers that property, not this review.\n\nThe root `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/config.rs#enum[AgentConfig]` derives `Serialize, Deserialize`, with `#[serde(tag = \"backend\", rename_all = \"lowercase\")]`. Its variants hold `BedrockConfig`, `OpenAiConfig` and `OllamaConfig`, which a reviewer reaches by following the types the marked root uses. Each of those also derives `Serialize, Deserialize` in the same file: `struct[BedrockConfig]`, `struct[OpenAiConfig]` and `struct[OllamaConfig]`. So do the nested types they use: `struct[BedrockCredentials]`, `struct[SecretString]` (a transparent newtype) and `enum[OptionValue]`. All of these are reached through the marked root, so they need no separate {impl} markers. No other root needs a reference, and no acknowledgments apply."
