        "status": "success",
        "rationale": "The requirement asks for Serde serialization and deserialization of `AgentConfig` and its backend config types, and for a round trip that preserves settings. It also says an application can embed them in its own configuration types.\n\n`mod[tests]/fn[all_backends_preserve_nested_settings_and_credentials]` in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/config.rs` covers the round trip:\n- It uses fully populated Bedrock, OpenAI and Ollama JSON values.\n- It deserializes each into `AgentConfig` and asserts the re-serialized value equals the input exactly.\n- It then round-trips through a JSON string and asserts the copy equals the original config.\n- `AgentConfig` wraps `BedrockConfig`, `OpenAiConfig` and `OllamaConfig`, so those types are exercised through it.\n- The Bedrock case covers `BedrockCredentials`, including `session_token`.\n- The inputs also cover nested option maps and arrays and every `OptionValue` variant: string, bool, integer, float, array and map.\n- The `backend` tag is checked by the equality assertion on every case.\n\n`mod[tests]/fn[configuration_debug_hides_credentials]` adds `SecretString` serialization and deserialization. It also checks that an unknown field is rejected.\n\nThe embedding obligation is satisfied by the Serde derives on `AgentConfig` and the config types, and the round-trip tests exercise those derives. Embedding adds no conditional or transformation of its own. I found no required behavior that the tests would let a violation through on.\n\nNo gaps found."
      }
